/* Bassiet book, Depth 12: The Gland Spires. Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c12-pit']={depth:12,title:'The High Spire',
epi:'At the top of the tallest spire a small gland gives orders to all the others. Someone has been whispering to it.',
c:['pituitary','water-balance','adrenal','thyroid','sex-hormones'],
S:[
{h:'The axis',p:[
'The hypothalamus speaks first. Its neurons release hormones into the portal veins of the pituitary stalk, a private low-pressure circulation that carries them straight to the anterior lobe. The **anterior pituitary** (adenohypophysis, from Rathke pouch, an upgrowth of oral ectoderm) answers with **ACTH** (cut, with MSH, from the precursor POMC), **TSH, LH and FSH** (glycoproteins that share one α subunit with hCG and differ by their β subunit), **GH** and **prolactin**. On H&E, GH and prolactin cells stain with acid dyes (acidophils); the glycoprotein and ACTH cells stain with basic dyes (basophils). The **posterior pituitary** (neurohypophysis, a downgrowth of the brain) makes nothing: it stores and releases **ADH** and **oxytocin** carried down axons from the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei.',
'Most axes run by **negative feedback**: the target hormone damps both the hypothalamus and the pituitary, so the pattern of high and low levels tells you where a fault sits. Prolactin is the exception: it is tonically **inhibited by dopamine**, so anything that cuts the stalk (a tumour, trauma, surgery) raises prolactin a little while every other anterior hormone falls. TRH and oestrogen stimulate prolactin; prolactin in turn suppresses GnRH, which is how breastfeeding and prolactinomas stop ovulation.',
'**Growth hormone** is released in pulses, mostly in deep sleep, and is driven by GHRH, ghrelin, hypoglycaemia, exercise and puberty; it is suppressed by somatostatin, glucose and IGF-1. It acts through a cytokine-type receptor (JAK2-STAT) and makes the liver release **IGF-1**, which carries most of the growth effect; GH itself is diabetogenic and lipolytic.'],
table:{cols:['Hypothalamus','Pituitary','Target'],rows:[['TRH','TSH (and prolactin)','thyroid'],['CRH','ACTH','adrenal cortex'],['GnRH (pulsatile)','LH, FSH','gonads'],['GHRH (↑) and somatostatin (↓)','GH','liver (IGF-1), bone, muscle'],['Dopamine','↓prolactin','breast'],['Supraoptic and paraventricular neurons','ADH, oxytocin (posterior lobe)','collecting duct and vessels; uterus and breast']]},
s:[['pharm','Continuous GnRH agonists (leuprorelin, also called leuprolide) first flare, then shut the axis down (prostate cancer, endometriosis, central precocious puberty); pulsatile GnRH restores fertility. Dopamine agonists (cabergoline, bromocriptine) shrink prolactinomas. Somatostatin analogues (octreotide) and pegvisomant (a GH receptor antagonist) treat acromegaly.']]},
{h:'Too much',p:[
'**Prolactinoma** (the commonest functioning pituitary adenoma): galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, infertility, low libido and erectile dysfunction (prolactin suppresses GnRH). Women notice early; men often present late with a large tumour, headache and bitemporal hemianopia. Other causes of high prolactin must be ruled out: pregnancy, D₂-blocking drugs (antipsychotics, metoclopramide), methyldopa, verapamil, primary hypothyroidism (high TRH), kidney failure, and stalk compression by any large mass. Cabergoline is first-line, even for large tumours.',
'**Acromegaly**: GH excess after the growth plates have closed: larger hands, feet and jaw (rings and shoes no longer fit), coarse face, frontal bossing, macroglossia, sweating, carpal tunnel, arthritis, sleep apnoea, diabetes, hypertension and cardiomyopathy (the commonest cause of death), and colon polyps. IGF-1 is raised, and GH fails to suppress after an oral glucose load. Before the plates close the same excess causes gigantism. Transsphenoidal surgery first, then drugs. **Cushing disease** is an ACTH-secreting adenoma (see the Twin Crowns); after both adrenals are removed for it, the unchecked adenoma can grow and darken the skin (**Nelson syndrome**). Rare **TSH-secreting adenomas** cause hyperthyroidism with a non-suppressed TSH.'],
s:[['path','A large pituitary tumour grows up out of the sella and presses on the crossing nasal fibres of the optic chiasm: bitemporal hemianopia. Craniopharyngioma (children, a remnant of Rathke pouch; calcified, cystic, filled with "machine-oil" fluid) does the same and can cause diabetes insipidus and growth failure. Pituitary adenomas are part of MEN1.']]},
{h:'Too little',p:[
'**Hypopituitarism**: tumours (the commonest cause), surgery, radiation, **Sheehan syndrome** (the pituitary enlarges in pregnancy but keeps its low-pressure blood supply, so a postpartum haemorrhage infarcts it: failure to lactate, no return of periods, fatigue, later low cortisol and thyroxine), **pituitary apoplexy** (sudden haemorrhage into an adenoma: thunderclap headache, visual loss, eye-movement palsies, and acute cortisol lack that needs hydrocortisone at once), empty sella, trauma and infiltration (sarcoid, haemochromatosis). GH and gonadotrophins usually fail first. Replace cortisol before thyroxine, because thyroxine speeds cortisol clearance and can tip a cortisol-deficient patient into adrenal crisis. **Kallmann syndrome**: GnRH neurons fail to migrate from the olfactory placode, so low GnRH, absent puberty and a lost sense of smell.',
'**Diabetes insipidus**: **central** (no ADH: tumours, trauma, surgery, infiltration, idiopathic) or **nephrogenic** (no response to ADH: lithium, demeclocycline, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia, V₂ receptor or aquaporin-2 mutations). Large volumes of dilute urine, high-normal sodium and thirst; if the patient cannot drink, sodium climbs. The **water deprivation test** separates the causes (table), and copeptin, made with ADH from the same precursor, is a stable stand-in for measuring ADH. The opposite state, **SIADH** (euvolaemic hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine, from small cell lung cancer, CNS and lung disease, and drugs such as SSRIs, carbamazepine and cyclophosphamide), is dealt with in the Salt Ledgers.'],
table:{cols:['Test','Primary polydipsia','Central DI','Nephrogenic DI'],rows:[['Plasma sodium at rest','low-normal','high-normal','high-normal'],['Urine after water deprivation','concentrates','stays dilute','stays dilute'],['Urine after desmopressin','little further change','concentrates by more than 50%','little or no change'],['ADH or copeptin','low','low','high']]},
key:'Deprive, then give desmopressin. Concentrates on deprivation alone: the patient was drinking too much. Concentrates only after desmopressin: no ADH. Concentrates after neither: the kidney cannot hear ADH.',
s:[['phys','Oxytocin: milk let-down and uterine contraction; released by suckling and cervical stretch (Ferguson reflex). ADH: V₂ receptors in the collecting duct (Gs, cAMP, aquaporin-2 inserted into the luminal membrane) and V₁ receptors on vessels (Gq, vasoconstriction). ADH is released when plasma osmolality rises (osmoreceptors) or when volume falls by about 10% or more (baroreceptors).'],['pharm','Central DI: desmopressin (a selective V₂ agonist; nasal, oral or injected; watch for hyponatraemia). Nephrogenic DI: thiazides (mild volume loss raises proximal water reabsorption), amiloride for lithium-induced DI (it blocks lithium entry through ENaC), salt restriction and NSAIDs. SIADH: fluid restriction, then V₂ antagonists (tolvaptan, conivaptan) or demeclocycline.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 28-year-old woman started on risperidone develops amenorrhoea and milky nipple discharge. Mechanism?',o:['TRH excess from hypothyroidism','Pituitary apoplexy','D₂ blockade lifts prolactin','Direct ovarian toxicity'],a:2,d:'pharm',c:'pituitary',x:'Hypothalamic dopamine tonically inhibits lactotrophs through D₂ receptors; risperidone blocks D₂ in the tuberoinfundibular pathway, so prolactin rises, suppresses GnRH and stops ovulation. Hypothyroidism can raise prolactin through TRH, which is why TSH is checked, but the new drug is the obvious cause here.'},
{q:'A 45-year-old man needs bigger shoes and rings, has a coarse face and new diabetes. Best first test?',o:['Random GH level','IGF-1 level','Prolactin level','Pituitary MRI first'],a:1,d:'phys',c:'pituitary',x:'GH is released in pulses, so a random level is unreliable, while IGF-1 from the liver integrates GH secretion over the day and is the screening test. Confirm with failure of GH to suppress after an oral glucose load, then image; an MRI first risks finding an incidental adenoma before hormone excess is proved.'},
{q:'After a massive postpartum haemorrhage, a woman cannot breastfeed and her periods never return. Diagnosis?',o:['Prolactinoma','Asherman syndrome','Postpartum thyroiditis','Sheehan syndrome'],a:3,d:'path',c:'pituitary',x:'The pregnant pituitary is enlarged but keeps its low-pressure portal supply, so haemorrhagic shock infarcts it: no prolactin for lactation, no gonadotrophins, and later low ACTH and TSH. Asherman syndrome also stops periods after an obstetric event, but it is uterine scarring after curettage, and lactation is normal.'},
{q:'A man with panhypopituitarism after surgery is low in cortisol, thyroxine and testosterone. Which hormone is replaced first?',o:['Hydrocortisone','Thyroxine','Testosterone','Growth hormone'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'adrenal',x:'Thyroxine speeds cortisol clearance and raises metabolic demand, so giving it to someone who cannot make cortisol can cause adrenal crisis; hydrocortisone starts first, then levothyroxine. Mineralocorticoid is usually not needed in pituitary disease, because renin and angiotensin still drive aldosterone.'},
{q:'Polyuria; after 8 hours of water deprivation the urine stays dilute (osmolality below 300 mOsm/kg), then rises by more than 50% after desmopressin. Diagnosis?',o:['Nephrogenic DI','Primary polydipsia','Central DI','SIADH'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'water-balance',x:'Urine that cannot concentrate on deprivation but does after desmopressin means the kidney works and ADH is missing: central DI, with intact V₂ receptors. Nephrogenic DI barely responds to desmopressin, and primary polydipsia concentrates with deprivation alone because the patient can still make ADH.'},
{q:'A smoker with a central lung mass has sodium 121 mmol/L, low serum osmolality, urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg and normal volume status. Most likely mechanism?',o:['Excess water drinking','Low aldosterone from adrenal failure','Thiazide-induced sodium loss','Ectopic ADH'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'water-balance',x:'Small cell carcinoma can secrete ADH, which keeps aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct whatever the osmolality: water is retained, sodium is diluted and the urine is inappropriately concentrated while the patient looks euvolaemic (SIADH). Excess drinking would leave the urine maximally dilute (below about 100 mOsm/kg). Sodium is raised slowly to avoid osmotic demyelination.'}
]};

BOOK['c12-thy']={depth:12,title:'The Thyroid Beacon',
epi:'A beacon that sets how fast every furnace burns. It is flickering between too bright and too dim.',
c:['thyroid','thyroid-cancer','hypersensitivity','p53-rb'],
S:[
{h:'Making thyroid hormone',p:[
'The thyroid begins at the foramen caecum at the back of the tongue and descends to the neck along the **thyroglossal duct**, which should vanish. A remnant forms a **thyroglossal duct cyst**: a midline neck lump that moves up when the tongue is protruded or the patient swallows. A gland that never descends sits at the tongue base (lingual thyroid) and may be the only thyroid tissue. Follicular cells come from endoderm; the parafollicular **C cells** come from neural crest via the fourth pharyngeal pouch.',
'Iodide is pumped into follicular cells by the **Na⁺/I⁻ symporter**, oxidised by **thyroid peroxidase (TPO)**, attached to tyrosines on thyroglobulin (organification), and coupled to make **T4** (mostly) and **T3**. Peripheral **5′-deiodinase** converts T4 to the active T3, or to inactive reverse T3. Thyroid hormone binds nuclear receptors and raises metabolic rate, Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity, β₁ receptor numbers (heart rate and output), bone growth and brain development (hence intellectual disability in untreated congenital hypothyroidism). Most circulating hormone is bound to thyroxine-binding globulin, which rises in pregnancy and with oestrogen (total T4 up, free T4 normal) and falls in nephrotic syndrome and with androgens.',
'Iodide itself regulates the gland. A large iodine load briefly shuts down organification (**Wolff-Chaikoff effect**), and a normal gland escapes within days; a gland that cannot escape becomes hypothyroid. In an iodine-deficient gland or a nodular goitre, the same load can instead fuel uncontrolled hormone synthesis and thyrotoxicosis (**Jod-Basedow phenomenon**).'],
s:[['pharm','Propylthiouracil blocks TPO and peripheral 5′-deiodinase; it can cause severe liver failure and ANCA-positive vasculitis; used in the first trimester and in thyroid storm. Methimazole (and its prodrug carbimazole) blocks TPO; it is teratogenic in the first trimester (aplasia cutis). Both can cause agranulocytosis: stop the drug if a sore throat or fever appears and check the count. Radioactive iodine ablates the gland; it is contraindicated in pregnancy and can worsen Graves eye disease (give steroid cover). β-blockers ease symptoms, and propranolol also weakly blocks 5′-deiodinase.']]},
{h:'Reading the beacon',p:[
'Measure **TSH** first. Because the pituitary sees free hormone and responds steeply, TSH moves before free T4 does, so it is the best single screen. Then free T4 (and T3 if hyperthyroid) locates the fault: TSH and T4 moving in opposite directions means the thyroid is at fault (primary); moving in the same direction means the pituitary or the hypothalamus is (secondary or central).'],
table:{cols:['State','TSH','Free T4','Notes'],rows:[
['Primary hyperthyroidism','↓','↑','Graves, toxic nodule or multinodular goitre, thyroiditis, excess hormone'],
['Subclinical hyperthyroidism','↓','normal','atrial fibrillation and bone loss risk in older people'],
['Secondary hyperthyroidism','↑ or normal','↑','TSH-secreting adenoma; also thyroid hormone resistance'],
['Primary hypothyroidism','↑','↓','Hashimoto, iodine deficiency, after ablation or surgery'],
['Subclinical hypothyroidism','↑','normal','often anti-TPO positive; treat in pregnancy or when TSH is very high'],
['Secondary hypothyroidism','↓ or normal','↓','pituitary or hypothalamic disease; TSH cannot guide dosing'],
['Euthyroid sick syndrome','normal or ↓','normal or ↓','severe illness: ↓T3, ↑reverse T3; adaptive; do not treat'],
['TBG excess (pregnancy, oestrogen)','normal','normal','total T4 ↑; the patient is euthyroid']]}},
{h:'Hyperthyroidism',p:[
'Heat intolerance, weight loss with a good appetite, tremor, anxiety, palpitations, atrial fibrillation, diarrhoea, oligomenorrhoea, fine hair, warm moist skin, lid lag and proximal myopathy. **Graves disease**: IgG that stimulates the TSH receptor (type II hypersensitivity, the stimulating kind); diffuse goitre with a bruit, **ophthalmopathy** (T cells and antibodies activate orbital fibroblasts, which swell the muscles and fat with glycosaminoglycans: proptosis, diplopia; worse with smoking) and **pretibial myxoedema**; diffusely high radioiodine uptake. **Toxic multinodular goitre**: several autonomous nodules, patchy uptake. **Toxic adenoma**: one hot nodule with the rest of the gland suppressed (TSH receptor activating mutation).',
'**Thyroiditis** releases stored hormone from damaged follicles, so uptake is low and thyroglobulin is high: **subacute granulomatous (de Quervain)** follows a virus, with a painful tender gland, fever and high ESR, then a hypothyroid phase and recovery (NSAIDs); **painless and postpartum thyroiditis** are lymphocytic and often anti-TPO positive. Excess exogenous hormone also gives low uptake, but thyroglobulin is low. **Thyroid storm** (after surgery, infection, iodine contrast or childbirth in poorly controlled disease): fever, delirium, tachyarrhythmia and heart failure; give a β-blocker, PTU and steroids (PTU and steroids also block T4-to-T3 conversion), then iodine, at least an hour after the thionamide so the iodine is not used to build new hormone.'],
s:[['phys','In hyperthyroidism high T3 suppresses TSH; a low TSH with normal free T4 and T3 is subclinical hyperthyroidism. Thyroid hormone raises β₁ receptor numbers, which is why the heart races and why β-blockers help so quickly.'],['pharm','Graves eye disease that is active and threatening sight: high-dose steroids, orbital radiotherapy, and teprotumumab (an antibody to the IGF-1 receptor on orbital fibroblasts). Stopping smoking matters.']]},
{h:'Hypothyroidism',p:[
'Cold intolerance, weight gain, fatigue, low mood, constipation, dry skin, hair loss, menorrhagia, bradycardia, slow-relaxing reflexes, myopathy (raised CK), hyperlipidaemia, hyponatraemia, carpal tunnel and non-pitting oedema (glycosaminoglycans in the skin). **Hashimoto thyroiditis** (commonest cause where iodine is sufficient): anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies, a lymphocytic infiltrate with germinal centres and **Hürthle cells**; there may be an early transient hyperthyroid phase as follicles break; raised risk of **thyroid lymphoma** (a rapidly enlarging gland). Worldwide, **iodine deficiency** is the leading cause. **Congenital hypothyroidism** (thyroid dysgenesis most often): poor feeding, pot belly, pale puffy face, protruding umbilicus, macroglossia, prolonged jaundice and, untreated, intellectual disability; newborn screening exists to prevent it. **Myxoedema coma**: hypothermia, coma, bradycardia and hypoventilation; IV levothyroxine with hydrocortisone. **Riedel thyroiditis**: rock-hard fibrosis that can spread into the neck, IgG4-related.'],
s:[['pharm','Levothyroxine on an empty stomach; iron, calcium and PPIs reduce absorption; doses rise in pregnancy. Lithium causes hypothyroidism (and goitre) by blocking hormone release. Amiodarone (about 37% iodine by weight) can cause either state: hypothyroidism (Wolff-Chaikoff) or thyrotoxicosis, type 1 from iodine-fuelled synthesis in an abnormal gland (Jod-Basedow) and type 2 from destructive thyroiditis (steroids). Check thyroid tests before and during treatment.']]},
{h:'Nodules and cancer',p:[
'Nodules are common, and most are benign colloid nodules or adenomas. Concern rises with childhood neck irradiation, a family history of medullary cancer or MEN2, rapid growth, hoarseness, a hard fixed lump and nodes. Check **TSH** first: a low TSH points to a hot (hyperfunctioning) nodule, which is rarely malignant and is confirmed by an uptake scan. Otherwise ultrasound grades the nodule and **fine-needle aspiration** samples the suspicious ones.',
'Papillary and follicular cancers come from follicular cells, keep some ability to trap iodine, and are treated with thyroidectomy and then radioiodine to ablate remnants, with TSH kept low; thyroglobulin is the marker of recurrence. Medullary cancer comes from C cells and does not take up iodine, so radioiodine is useless; calcitonin and CEA follow it, and RET inhibitors (selpercatinib) treat advanced disease. Anaplastic cancer grows within weeks and invades the trachea and great vessels.'],
table:{cols:['Cancer','Clues'],rows:[
['Papillary (commonest, best prognosis)','empty "Orphan Annie eye" nuclei, nuclear grooves and pseudo-inclusions, psammoma bodies; childhood radiation; RET/PTC rearrangements, BRAF V600E; spreads to cervical lymph nodes'],
['Follicular','capsular and vascular invasion, so FNA cannot tell it from an adenoma; RAS, PAX8-PPARγ; spreads by blood to bone and lung; commoner where iodine is lacking'],
['Medullary','parafollicular C cells; calcitonin, amyloid stroma made from calcitonin; sporadic or MEN2A and 2B (RET): prophylactic thyroidectomy in carriers'],
['Anaplastic','older people, rapid, invades; TP53 loss; very poor prognosis'],
['Lymphoma','Hashimoto background; rapid painless enlargement; usually diffuse large B-cell or MALT type']]},
s:[['path','After thyroidectomy: hypocalcaemia (parathyroids removed or bruised), hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve, which also runs close to the inferior thyroid artery), and loss of high pitch (external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, beside the superior thyroid artery).']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 30-year-old woman has weight loss, proptosis, raised shin plaques and diffusely increased radioiodine uptake. Mechanism?',o:['TSH-receptor stimulating antibodies','TPO antibodies destroying the gland','Autonomous TSH-independent nodule','Granulomatous viral thyroiditis'],a:0,d:'path',c:'thyroid',x:'Graves disease is a type II hypersensitivity in which IgG stimulates the TSH receptor, so the whole gland is driven (diffuse uptake), and the same autoimmunity activates orbital and pretibial fibroblasts. TPO antibodies destroy the gland in Hashimoto thyroiditis and are often present in Graves too, but they are not what makes it overactive.'},
{q:'A nurse has thyrotoxicosis, a small non-tender gland, low radioiodine uptake and a very low serum thyroglobulin. Cause?',o:['Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis','Graves disease','Toxic adenoma','Exogenous thyroid hormone'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'thyroid',x:'Taking thyroid hormone suppresses TSH, so the gland shrinks, takes up no iodine and stops releasing thyroglobulin. Thyroiditis also gives low uptake, but damaged follicles leak thyroglobulin, so it is high, and subacute thyroiditis makes the gland tender.'},
{q:'A woman with Graves disease is 7 weeks pregnant. Which antithyroid drug is preferred until the end of the first trimester?',o:['Methimazole','Propylthiouracil','Radioactive iodine','Carbimazole'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'thyroid',x:'Methimazole and its prodrug carbimazole can cause aplasia cutis and other embryopathy in the first trimester, so propylthiouracil is used until about 16 weeks; many then switch back because PTU can cause severe liver failure. Radioactive iodine is contraindicated in pregnancy because it would ablate the fetal thyroid.'},
{q:'A woman irradiated to the neck as a child has a thyroid nodule. FNA shows psammoma bodies and pale, empty-looking nuclei with grooves. Diagnosis?',o:['Follicular carcinoma','Medullary carcinoma','Papillary carcinoma','Anaplastic carcinoma'],a:2,d:'path',c:'thyroid-cancer',x:'Papillary carcinoma is diagnosed by its nuclei (clear chromatin, grooves, pseudo-inclusions), and psammoma bodies are calcified dead papillae; radiation and RET/PTC or BRAF changes drive it, it spreads to nodes, and its prognosis is excellent. Follicular carcinoma cannot be diagnosed on FNA, because only capsular or vascular invasion in the resected nodule proves it.'},
{q:'A thyroid tumour has amyloid stroma and a high calcitonin; the patient\'s brother had a phaeochromocytoma. Mutated gene?',o:['BRAF','RET','RAS','TP53'],a:1,d:'path',c:'thyroid-cancer',x:'Medullary carcinoma comes from parafollicular C cells, so calcitonin is high and the amyloid is built from calcitonin; with a relative\'s phaeochromocytoma this is MEN2, a gain-of-function RET mutation, and carriers have the thyroid removed early. BRAF and RAS drive papillary and follicular tumours instead.'},
{q:'A patient in intensive care with septic shock has low free T3, high reverse T3 and a normal TSH. Best management of these thyroid results?',o:['Start levothyroxine now','Start liothyronine (T3) now','Treat nothing; recheck later','Give PTU to block deiodinase'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'thyroid',x:'Severe illness, cytokines and fasting shift deiodination away from T3 toward reverse T3, and TSH may be normal or low: euthyroid sick (non-thyroidal illness) syndrome. It is adaptive, so the tests are repeated after recovery rather than treated; giving hormone has not been shown to help. PTU would lower T3 further.'}
]};

BOOK['c12-adr']={depth:12,title:'The Twin Crowns',
epi:'Two crowns on the kidneys. Their rims brew the slow hormones of salt, fuel and sex. Their cores scream adrenaline.',
c:['adrenal','cushing','hyperaldosteronism','catecholamines','hypertension','raas','potassium'],
S:[
{h:'The cortex in layers',p:[
'Read each crown from the rim inward. The outer band, **zona glomerulosa**, keeps the salt: it makes aldosterone, driven by angiotensin II and by high K⁺, and barely by ACTH. The middle band, **zona fasciculata**, keeps the fuel: it makes cortisol under ACTH. The inner band, **zona reticularis**, makes the weak androgens (DHEA, androstenedione), also under ACTH. At the core sits the **medulla**, made of neural crest chromaffin cells that act as a sympathetic ganglion and make adrenaline and noradrenaline.',
'All three cortical hormones come from cholesterol. Moving cholesterol into the mitochondrion (StAR protein) and cutting its side chain (desmolase, CYP11A1, the ACTH-driven step) give pregnenolone. From there **21-hydroxylase** and **11β-hydroxylase** lead to aldosterone and cortisol, and **17α-hydroxylase** opens the branch toward cortisol and the androgens. Any enzyme block therefore lowers what lies after it and floods the branches before it, which is the whole logic of **congenital adrenal hyperplasia** (CAH): in every form cortisol is low, ACTH is high and the adrenals enlarge.',
'**Cortisol** raises glucose (gluconeogenesis, insulin resistance), breaks down muscle protein and redistributes fat, suppresses immunity and inflammation (inhibits phospholipase A₂ and NF-κB; lymphocytes and eosinophils fall while neutrophils rise), keeps blood pressure up (it up-regulates α₁ receptors on arterioles), weakens bone and collagen, and in excess overwhelms renal 11β-HSD2 and acts on mineralocorticoid receptors. It peaks in the early morning.'],
table:{cols:['Congenital adrenal hyperplasia','Cortisol / renin','Mineralocorticoid','Androgens','Clues'],rows:[
['21-hydroxylase (commonest, over 90%)','↓ / ↑','aldosterone ↓ (salt wasting)','↑','ambiguous genitalia in 46,XX infants; salt-wasting crisis in the second week, missed in boys whose genitals look normal; ↑17-hydroxyprogesterone; mild non-classic form gives hirsutism in young women'],
['11β-hydroxylase','↓ / ↓','aldosterone ↓ but 11-deoxycorticosterone ↑ (net excess)','↑','virilisation with hypertension and low K⁺'],
['17α-hydroxylase','↓ / ↓','aldosterone ↓ but 11-deoxycorticosterone ↑','↓','hypertension, low K⁺; 46,XY infants look female, 46,XX girls have no puberty; corticosterone covers the cortisol lack']]},
s:[['pharm','Ketoconazole, metyrapone and osilodrostat block steroid-making enzymes (11β-hydroxylase above all); abiraterone blocks 17α-hydroxylase and 17,20-lyase in prostate cancer, so it is given with prednisone to cover the cortisol loss. CAH is treated with glucocorticoid, which also suppresses the excess ACTH, plus fludrocortisone in salt wasters.']]},
{h:'Cushing syndrome',p:[
'Too much cortisol for too long: central obesity, moon face, dorsocervical fat pad, purple striae wider than 1 cm, thin skin and bruising, proximal myopathy, hypertension, diabetes, osteoporosis, depression or psychosis, infections and menstrual disturbance; children stop growing. Causes: **exogenous glucocorticoids** (by far the commonest), a **pituitary ACTH adenoma** (Cushing disease, the commonest endogenous cause), **ectopic ACTH** (small cell lung cancer, bronchial carcinoids; very high ACTH, fast onset, hyperpigmentation and low K⁺), and an **adrenal adenoma, carcinoma or nodular hyperplasia** (ACTH suppressed, so the other adrenal atrophies).',
'Screen with 24-hour urinary free cortisol, late-night salivary cortisol, or the 1 mg overnight (low-dose) dexamethasone suppression test. Then measure ACTH: low → adrenal source (CT adrenals). High → pituitary or ectopic: on high-dose dexamethasone a pituitary adenoma suppresses and an ectopic source does not; after CRH a pituitary adenoma raises ACTH further and an ectopic source does not. MRI pituitary, and inferior petrosal sinus sampling if the source is still unclear.'],
key:'ACTH low: the adrenal itself (or steroids taken). ACTH high, bowing to high-dose dexamethasone and rising after CRH: the pituitary. ACTH high and ignoring both: an ectopic tumour.',
s:[['pharm','Long-term steroids suppress the axis: taper slowly, and cover illness and surgery with extra doses. Steroid side effects: avascular necrosis of the femoral head, cataracts, glaucoma, hyperglycaemia, psychosis, myopathy, osteoporosis, and peptic ulcers with NSAIDs. Cortisol-lowering drugs when surgery fails: ketoconazole, metyrapone and osilodrostat (steroid synthesis), mifepristone (glucocorticoid receptor blocker), pasireotide (somatostatin analogue acting on the pituitary adenoma).']]},
{h:'Adrenal insufficiency',p:[
'**Primary (Addison disease)**: autoimmune adrenalitis (commonest in the developed world, often with other autoimmune endocrine disease), TB (worldwide), metastases, HIV-related infections, and haemorrhage (**Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome** with meningococcaemia; also anticoagulants and antiphospholipid syndrome). The whole cortex is lost, so both cortisol and aldosterone are low: fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, hypotension, **hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia**, mild metabolic acidosis, hypoglycaemia, eosinophilia, salt craving and **hyperpigmentation** of creases, scars and gums (ACTH and MSH both come from POMC). **Secondary** (pituitary) and tertiary (hypothalamic, most often after long steroid use) insufficiency spare aldosterone, so potassium is normal, and there is no hyperpigmentation because ACTH is low.',
'Diagnosis: low morning cortisol that fails to rise after synthetic ACTH (cosyntropin); then ACTH: high in primary, low or normal in secondary. **Adrenal crisis** (shock, vomiting, confusion, often triggered by infection or by stopping steroids): IV hydrocortisone at once, saline and glucose, without waiting for results. Patients double their dose during illness (sick-day rules) and carry injectable hydrocortisone.'],
s:[['phys','Why the potassium differs: aldosterone is driven mainly by angiotensin II and potassium, not ACTH. So pituitary failure leaves the glomerulosa working, while adrenal destruction removes it, and K⁺ and H⁺ can no longer be secreted in the collecting duct.']]},
{h:'Too much salt hormone',p:[
'Aldosterone acts on principal cells of the collecting duct: more ENaC channels let Na⁺ in, more Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps it out, and K⁺ (and, via intercalated cells, H⁺) is lost in the urine. Excess therefore gives hypertension, hypokalaemia (weakness, polyuria) and metabolic alkalosis, but no oedema, because after a few days the extra volume raises pressure natriuresis and ANP and sodium balance resets (aldosterone escape). **Primary hyperaldosteronism** (Conn: an adenoma or bilateral hyperplasia) is the commonest endocrine cause of hypertension; screen with the aldosterone:renin ratio, confirm with salt loading, and use adrenal vein sampling to find a one-sided source.',
'Several imitators make the same picture with low renin and low aldosterone: something other than aldosterone is switching on the pathway. Sorting by renin and aldosterone finds them quickly.'],
table:{cols:['Condition','Renin','Aldosterone','Clue'],rows:[
['Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn)','↓','↑','adenoma or bilateral hyperplasia; spironolactone or eplerenone; remove an adenoma'],
['Secondary hyperaldosteronism','↑','↑','renal artery stenosis, heart failure, cirrhosis, diuretics, renin-secreting tumour'],
['Liddle syndrome','↓','↓','autosomal dominant gain of ENaC function; amiloride or triamterene, not spironolactone'],
['Apparent mineralocorticoid excess, liquorice','↓','↓','11β-HSD2 lost or blocked (glycyrrhetinic acid), so cortisol switches on the mineralocorticoid receptor'],
['11β- or 17α-hydroxylase CAH','↓','↓','11-deoxycorticosterone acts as the mineralocorticoid'],
['Severe Cushing (ectopic ACTH)','↓','normal or ↓','cortisol floods past 11β-HSD2']]}},
{h:'The medulla',p:[
'**Phaeochromocytoma**: a tumour of chromaffin cells, in the adrenal medulla or, as a paraganglioma, in sympathetic ganglia anywhere from the skull base to the bladder. The old "rule of 10s" is outdated: up to a third are hereditary. Spells of headache, sweating, palpitations and pallor with severe, often paroxysmal hypertension, triggered by pressure on the tumour, anaesthesia or some drugs. Associations: MEN2A and 2B (RET), von Hippel-Lindau, NF1 and SDH mutations. Diagnosis: plasma free or urinary fractionated **metanephrines**, then imaging. Treat with **α-blockade first** (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin, with fluid and salt to refill the contracted circulation), then β-blockade for tachycardia, then surgery.',
'**Neuroblastoma**: the commonest extracranial solid tumour of early childhood, from neural crest cells of the adrenal medulla or the sympathetic chain: an abdominal mass that crosses the midline, periorbital bruising from orbital spread, opsoclonus-myoclonus, raised homovanillic and vanillylmandelic acids in the urine, MYCN amplification (worse prognosis), and Homer-Wright rosettes; infants can regress spontaneously. Hypertension is less common than in phaeochromocytoma.'],
s:[['bio','Tyrosine → DOPA (tyrosine hydroxylase, rate-limiting) → dopamine → noradrenaline → adrenaline (PNMT, induced by the cortisol that bathes the medulla from the cortex above it). COMT inside the tumour turns noradrenaline and adrenaline into normetanephrine and metanephrine continuously, which is why metanephrines are raised even between spells; MAO and COMT together give VMA, and dopamine gives HVA.'],['pharm','Phenoxybenzamine binds α receptors irreversibly, so a surge of catecholamines cannot overcome it. Metyrosine blocks tyrosine hydroxylase and lowers synthesis before surgery. Never start a β-blocker alone: it removes β₂ vasodilation and leaves α₁ vasoconstriction unopposed.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A woman has weight loss, darkened palmar creases, BP 88/50 mmHg, Na⁺ 128 and K⁺ 5.9 mmol/L. Diagnosis?',o:['Secondary adrenal insufficiency','Primary adrenal insufficiency','Cushing disease','Conn syndrome'],a:1,d:'path',c:'adrenal',x:'In primary (Addison) failure the whole cortex is gone, so aldosterone is lost too (low sodium, high potassium, low pressure), and the unchecked pituitary makes ACTH with MSH from POMC, darkening the creases. Secondary insufficiency spares aldosterone because renin still drives it, and its ACTH is low, so there is no hyperpigmentation and potassium is normal.'},
{q:'A smoker has Cushing features, hypokalaemia and high ACTH; cortisol does not suppress on high-dose dexamethasone and ACTH does not rise after CRH. Source?',o:['Pituitary adenoma','Adrenal adenoma','Exogenous steroids','Ectopic ACTH'],a:3,d:'phys',c:'cushing',x:'Pituitary corticotroph adenomas keep some feedback, so high-dose dexamethasone suppresses them and CRH raises their ACTH further; ectopic sources such as small cell lung cancer ignore both. An adrenal adenoma and exogenous steroids both give a low ACTH. The very high cortisol swamps renal 11β-HSD2, so it acts on mineralocorticoid receptors and potassium falls.'},
{q:'A newborn girl has ambiguous genitalia and, at 2 weeks, vomiting with Na⁺ 124 and K⁺ 7.0 mmol/L. Which hormone is raised in her blood?',o:['17-hydroxyprogesterone','11-deoxycorticosterone','Cortisol','Aldosterone'],a:0,d:'bio',c:'hyperaldosteronism',x:'Without 21-hydroxylase the cortex cannot make cortisol or aldosterone, so ACTH rises, the glands enlarge and the trapped precursor 17-hydroxyprogesterone is shunted into androgens: a virilised girl with a salt-wasting crisis. 11-Deoxycorticosterone is raised in 11β-hydroxylase deficiency, which causes hypertension and low potassium instead.'},
{q:'A 40-year-old has resistant hypertension, K⁺ 2.9 mmol/L, metabolic alkalosis, high aldosterone and suppressed renin. Diagnosis?',o:['Fibromuscular renal artery stenosis','Liddle syndrome','Primary hyperaldosteronism','Phaeochromocytoma'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'hyperaldosteronism',x:'High aldosterone with low renin means the adrenal is making it autonomously (an adenoma or bilateral hyperplasia), so the aldosterone:renin ratio is high. Renal artery stenosis raises renin and aldosterone together, and Liddle syndrome has low renin with low aldosterone because the ENaC channel itself is overactive.'},
{q:'A 35-year-old has spells of pounding headache, sweating and palpitations, with BP 220/120 mmHg during an attack. Best first test?',o:['Random serum catecholamines','Plasma free metanephrines','Morning serum cortisol','Plasma renin activity'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'catecholamines',x:'Phaeochromocytomas release catecholamines in bursts but convert them by COMT inside the tumour continuously, so plasma free (or urinary fractionated) metanephrines are raised even between spells. Random catecholamines miss the episodes. Treatment starts with α-blockade, then β-blockade, then surgery.'},
{q:'Before surgery for a phaeochromocytoma, why must α-blockade start before any β-blocker?',o:['β-blockade leaves α tone unopposed','β-blockade first causes bronchospasm','α-blockers are slower to act','β-blockers trigger tumour release'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'catecholamines',x:'Circulating catecholamines act on α₁ receptors (constriction) and β₂ receptors (dilation of muscle arterioles); blocking β alone removes the dilation, so α₁ vasoconstriction runs unopposed and pressure can soar. Phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin goes first with fluid and salt to refill the circulation, and a β-blocker is added for tachycardia.'}
]};

BOOK['c12-panc']={depth:12,title:'The Sugar Islands',
epi:'Small islands in the pancreas keep the sugar in the blood within narrow banks. Some islands have been burned out.',
c:['insulin-glucagon','diabetes','diabetes-drugs','hypoglycaemia','glycogen-storage','acid-base'],
S:[
{h:'Insulin and glucagon',p:[
'**β cells** make preproinsulin, fold it as proinsulin and cut it into insulin and **C-peptide**, released together in equal amounts. They sense glucose through GLUT2 and glucokinase: more glucose means more ATP, which closes **K-ATP channels**, depolarises the cell, opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels and releases insulin. Insulin acts through a **receptor tyrosine kinase**: the PI3K arm moves GLUT4 to the surface of muscle and fat (brain, liver, kidney, red cells and β cells use other GLUTs and need no insulin) and switches on storage; the MAPK arm drives growth. Glycogen, fat and protein are built, lipolysis, ketogenesis and gluconeogenesis are switched off, and **K⁺ moves into cells**.',
'**Glucagon** (α cells) acts on the liver alone, through Gs, cAMP and protein kinase A: glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis (by lifting the malonyl-CoA brake on fatty acid entry into mitochondria). Adrenaline, cortisol and GH also raise glucose and join glucagon as the counter-regulatory hormones. After a meal the insulin:glucagon ratio is high and the body stores; in fasting it is low and the body burns fat and makes glucose and ketones. Diabetes is a body stuck in the fasting state even while glucose is high.'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Mechanism','Main issues'],rows:[
['Insulin (rapid: lispro, aspart; intermediate: NPH; long: glargine, detemir, degludec)','replaces insulin','hypoglycaemia, weight gain, hypokalaemia, lipohypertrophy at injection sites'],
['Metformin','↓hepatic gluconeogenesis, ↑sensitivity (AMP kinase)','first-line in type 2; GI upset, lactic acidosis (avoid if eGFR below 30, hold for iodinated contrast), B12 deficiency; weight neutral; no hypoglycaemia alone'],
['Sulfonylureas (glipizide, gliclazide, glibenclamide, also called glyburide)','close K-ATP channels (SUR1) → insulin release','hypoglycaemia (long-acting, renally cleared agents in older people), weight gain'],
['Meglitinides (repaglinide)','close K-ATP channels, short acting','taken with meals; hypoglycaemia'],
['Thiazolidinediones (pioglitazone)','PPAR-γ: ↑insulin sensitivity, fat redistribution','weight gain, fluid retention (avoid in heart failure), fractures, bladder cancer'],
['GLP-1 agonists (semaglutide, liraglutide); dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide','↑glucose-dependent insulin, ↓glucagon, slow emptying, satiety','weight loss, nausea, pancreatitis, gallstones; cardiovascular benefit (semaglutide, liraglutide); thyroid C-cell tumours in rodents: avoid with personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN2'],
['DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin)','prolong endogenous GLP-1 and GIP','weight neutral; mild; rarely pancreatitis and joint pain'],
['SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin)','block proximal glucose reabsorption: glycosuria','genital fungal infections, UTIs, euglycaemic DKA, volume loss; heart failure and kidney benefit'],
['Amylin analogue (pramlintide)','↓glucagon, slows emptying, satiety','type 1 and 2 with mealtime insulin; hypoglycaemia'],
['α-glucosidase inhibitors (acarbose)','slow carbohydrate digestion at the brush border','flatulence, diarrhoea']]}},
{h:'Diabetes',p:[
'**Type 1**: T-cell destruction of β cells (antibodies to GAD65, insulin, IA-2 and ZnT8 mark it; HLA-DR3 and DR4), usually young and lean, absolute insulin lack, prone to **DKA**. **Type 2**: insulin resistance (obesity, inactivity) with progressive β-cell failure, a stronger family link than type 1, and **amyloid** (amylin) deposited in the islets; prone to **hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state** (HHS). **MODY** (maturity-onset diabetes of the young): autosomal dominant single-gene diabetes in lean young people with a strong family history; glucokinase mutations give mild stable hyperglycaemia needing no treatment, HNF1A mutations respond to sulfonylureas. **Gestational diabetes**: placental hormones (hPL) raise insulin resistance in the second half of pregnancy; the fetus makes extra insulin, grows large and becomes hypoglycaemic after birth.',
'**DKA**: insulin lack lets fat break down, the liver turns fatty acids into ketones (β-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate), and a high anion gap acidosis follows, with Kussmaul breathing, abdominal pain, vomiting, fruity breath and an osmotic diuresis that dehydrates. Serum K⁺ is often high on arrival (acidosis and insulin lack shift it out of cells) even though total body potassium is depleted; insulin drives it back in. Treat with fluids, insulin, then potassium as it falls; more detail is in the Acid Wells. **HHS** has very high glucose and osmolality with little ketosis (enough insulin to stop lipolysis), in older patients with confusion and severe dehydration.',
'Diagnosis: fasting glucose ≥ 7.0 mmol/L (126 mg/dL), HbA1c ≥ 6.5% (48 mmol/mol), 2-hour OGTT ≥ 11.1 mmol/L (200 mg/dL), or random ≥ 11.1 mmol/L with symptoms.'],
table:{cols:['Complication','Mechanism'],rows:[
['Nephropathy','glomerular hyperfiltration, basement membrane thickening, nodular sclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson), efferent hyalinosis; albuminuria first; ACE inhibitors and SGLT2 inhibitors slow it'],
['Retinopathy','microaneurysms, haemorrhages and exudates, then neovascularisation driven by VEGF; leading cause of blindness in working-age adults'],
['Neuropathy','sorbitol accumulation (aldose reductase) in Schwann cells and microvascular injury; stocking-glove loss, autonomic (gastroparesis, postural hypotension, erectile dysfunction), foot ulcers'],
['Cataracts','sorbitol in the lens'],
['Macrovascular','atherosclerosis: MI (the commonest cause of death), stroke, peripheral arterial disease'],
['Infections','mucormycosis (ketoacidosis), malignant otitis externa (Pseudomonas), emphysematous pyelonephritis']]},
key:'Non-enzymatic glycation of proteins (HbA1c, basement membrane collagen) and the sorbitol pathway drive most long-term damage.',
s:[['path','In type 2 diabetes the islets fill with amyloid made from amylin, which is co-secreted with insulin, and β-cell function has roughly halved by the time of diagnosis. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of afferent and efferent arterioles is the small-vessel signature of diabetes.']]},
{h:'Hypoglycaemia and stores',p:[
'Hypoglycaemia first sounds the sympathetic alarm (sweating, tremor, hunger, palpitations), then starves the brain (confusion, odd behaviour, seizures, coma). **Whipple triad**: symptoms, a low measured glucose, and relief when glucose is given. **High insulin with high C-peptide**: insulinoma or a sulfonylurea or meglitinide (screen the blood for the drug). **High insulin with low C-peptide**: injected insulin (often a relative or carer of someone with diabetes). Other causes: alcohol (blocks gluconeogenesis because NADH is high), adrenal insufficiency, liver failure, sepsis, critical illness and inborn errors. Repeated episodes blunt the warning symptoms (hypoglycaemia unawareness), and β-blockers mask the tremor and palpitations.',
'During fasting, liver glycogen lasts about a day; then gluconeogenesis (from lactate, alanine and glycerol) and, after a few days, ketones supply the brain. The glycogen storage diseases break this chain. Liver forms cause fasting hypoglycaemia and a big liver; muscle forms cause weakness and cramps with exercise.'],
table:{cols:['Glycogen storage disease','Enzyme','Clues'],rows:[['Von Gierke (I)','glucose-6-phosphatase','severe fasting hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis, hepatomegaly, high urate and triglycerides; frequent feeds, cornstarch'],['Pompe (II)','lysosomal acid α-glucosidase (acid maltase)','cardiomegaly, hypotonia in infants; enzyme replacement'],['Cori (III)','debranching enzyme','milder, normal lactate, abnormal short-branched glycogen'],['McArdle (V)','muscle glycogen phosphorylase','exercise cramps, myoglobinuria, second-wind phenomenon; lactate fails to rise with exercise']]},
s:[['pharm','Sulfonylurea overdose: glucose, then octreotide, which stops further insulin release. Severe hypoglycaemia without IV access: glucagon injection or nasal glucagon, which works only if liver glycogen remains (not in starvation or alcohol excess).']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'The wife of a man with type 1 diabetes has recurrent hypoglycaemia; during an episode her insulin is high and her C-peptide is very low. Cause?',o:['Insulinoma','Sulfonylurea ingestion','Exogenous insulin','Adrenal insufficiency'],a:2,d:'bio',c:'hypoglycaemia',x:'Proinsulin is cut into insulin and C-peptide in equal amounts, so insulin from her own β cells, whether driven by an insulinoma or a sulfonylurea, always comes with C-peptide. Injected insulin has none and suppresses her own β cells, so C-peptide falls; a drug screen separates a sulfonylurea from an insulinoma, which both raise C-peptide.'},
{q:'Which diabetes drug closes β-cell K-ATP channels and so can cause prolonged hypoglycaemia in an older patient with kidney disease?',o:['Metformin','Pioglitazone','Sitagliptin','Glipizide'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'diabetes-drugs',x:'Sulfonylureas bind SUR1 on the K-ATP channel and close it as ATP would, depolarising the β cell and releasing insulin whatever the glucose, so hypoglycaemia is their main danger, worse with long-acting, renally cleared agents. Metformin lowers hepatic glucose output and does not cause hypoglycaemia on its own.'},
{q:'A man with type 2 diabetes on empagliflozin vomits after surgery; glucose is 9 mmol/L, with a high anion gap acidosis and high blood ketones. Diagnosis?',o:['Euglycaemic DKA','Lactic acidosis','Starvation ketosis','Hyperosmolar state'],a:0,d:'pharm',c:'diabetes-drugs',x:'SGLT2 inhibitors keep glucose down by pouring it into the urine, while low insulin and high glucagon still drive ketogenesis, so DKA develops with near-normal glucose and is easily missed. Starvation ketosis causes only a mild acidosis, and the hyperosmolar state has very high glucose with little ketosis.'},
{q:'Diabetic neuropathy and cataracts both arise partly from which pathway, in cells that take up glucose without insulin?',o:['Glycation only','Sorbitol accumulation','Islet amyloid deposition','Autoimmunity'],a:1,d:'bio',c:'diabetes',x:'In Schwann cells, lens and retina glucose enters freely and aldose reductase reduces it to sorbitol; these cells have little sorbitol dehydrogenase, so sorbitol builds up, draws in water and uses up NADPH. Non-enzymatic glycation also damages tissue, but it is not the osmotic mechanism behind the cataract.'},
{q:'An infant has severe fasting hypoglycaemia, a large liver, lactic acidosis and high urate. Deficient enzyme?',o:['Debranching enzyme','Muscle phosphorylase','Glucose-6-phosphatase','Lysosomal acid maltase'],a:2,d:'bio',c:'glycogen-storage',x:'Von Gierke disease: without glucose-6-phosphatase the liver cannot release free glucose from either glycogen or gluconeogenesis, so fasting hypoglycaemia is severe, and the trapped glucose-6-phosphate is pushed into lactate, triglycerides and urate. Debranching deficiency (Cori) is milder, with normal lactate, because gluconeogenesis still works.'},
{q:'A woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes is offered semaglutide. Which history makes it contraindicated?',o:['Previous gestational diabetes','MEN2 in her family','Osteoporosis on alendronate','Stage 2 chronic kidney disease'],a:1,d:'pharm',c:'diabetes-drugs',x:'GLP-1 agonists caused C-cell (medullary) thyroid tumours in rodents, so a personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN2 rules them out, and pancreatitis is another caution. Mild kidney disease is no bar, and these drugs, like the dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide, give weight loss and, for semaglutide, cardiovascular benefit.'}
]};

BOOK['c12-ca']={depth:12,title:'The Bone Clerks',
epi:'Small clerks behind the thyroid keep the calcium ledger. They take from bone when the blood runs short.',
c:['calcium-pth','vitamins','men','bone-disease','osteoporosis'],
S:[
{h:'Three hormones',p:[
'About 45% of plasma calcium is free (ionised), the only part that acts and is sensed; about 40% is bound to albumin and the rest to anions. Low albumin lowers the total but not the ionised calcium, while **alkalosis** makes albumin bind more calcium, so hyperventilation can cause tingling and tetany with a normal total level.',
'**PTH** (chief cells; released within seconds when ionised Ca²⁺ falls, sensed by the calcium-sensing receptor) raises Ca²⁺ in three places: in bone it acts on osteoblasts, which raise RANKL and lower osteoprotegerin, so osteoclasts resorb; in the distal tubule it increases Ca²⁺ reabsorption; in the proximal tubule it cuts phosphate reabsorption (phosphaturia) and switches on **1α-hydroxylase** to make calcitriol. Continuous high PTH thins bone, while brief daily pulses (teriparatide) build it. **Calcitriol** (1,25-(OH)₂D) raises gut absorption of both Ca²⁺ and phosphate. **Calcitonin** (C cells) lowers Ca²⁺ slightly and matters little in humans. **FGF23** (from osteocytes) lowers phosphate and calcitriol. Low magnesium blocks both PTH release and its action, so hypocalcaemia will not correct until magnesium is replaced.',
'**Primary hyperparathyroidism** is usually a single adenoma (sometimes hyperplasia, as in MEN1) and is now mostly found on routine blood tests. When the clerks overspend, the ledger shows in four places: the kidneys (stones, polyuria and thirst), the bones (pain, fractures, and in severe disease osteitis fibrosa cystica with subperiosteal resorption and brown tumours), the gut (constipation, ulcers, pancreatitis) and the mind (low mood, poor concentration, confusion). Surgery cures it.'],
table:{cols:['Condition','Ca²⁺','PO₄³⁻','PTH','Notes'],rows:[
['Primary hyperparathyroidism','↑','↓','↑','adenoma (commonest); urinary calcium high; stones, bone loss, abdominal and mental symptoms'],
['Secondary (CKD)','↓ or normal','↑','↑','phosphate retention and low calcitriol; renal osteodystrophy'],
['Secondary (vitamin D deficiency)','↓ or normal','↓','↑','osteomalacia or rickets'],
['Tertiary','↑','↑','↑↑','glands become autonomous after long-standing CKD'],
['Hypoparathyroidism','↓','↑','↓','after thyroid or parathyroid surgery, DiGeorge syndrome, autoimmune'],
['Pseudohypoparathyroidism (Albright hereditary osteodystrophy)','↓','↑','↑','end-organ resistance (maternal GNAS, type 1A); short 4th and 5th metacarpals, short stature, round face, obesity. Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism (paternal GNAS): the same body with normal calcium and PTH'],
['Malignancy (PTHrP)','↑','↓','↓','squamous cell lung, renal, breast, bladder cancer'],
['Vitamin D excess or granulomas','↑','↑','↓','sarcoid, TB and lymphoma make calcitriol themselves; vitamin D overdose'],
['Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia','↑','normal','normal or ↑','inactivating CaSR mutation (autosomal dominant); very low urinary calcium; harmless, no surgery']]},
s:[['pharm','Severe hypercalcaemia: IV saline first, then calcitonin (works within hours, fades in days) and a bisphosphonate (zoledronic acid; works over 2–4 days); loop diuretics only after volume is restored; steroids for granulomas and lymphoma. Bisphosphonates are pyrophosphate analogues that bind bone mineral and trigger osteoclast apoptosis. Cinacalcet (a calcimimetic that sensitises the CaSR) lowers PTH. Denosumab is an antibody to RANKL.']]},
{h:'Vitamin D',p:[
'Skin makes cholecalciferol (D₃) from 7-dehydrocholesterol under UVB light; diet adds D₂ (plants and fungi) and D₃ (oily fish, fortified foods). The liver adds the first hydroxyl group to make **25-OH D**, the storage form with a half-life of weeks, which is what is measured. The kidney adds the second, through **1α-hydroxylase**, to make the active **1,25-(OH)₂D**; PTH and low phosphate switch this step on, and FGF23 and high calcitriol switch it off. Calcitriol acts on a nuclear receptor (VDR): it builds calcium channels and calbindin in the gut, helps PTH release calcium from bone, and feeds back to suppress PTH.',
'Deficiency: little sun, dark skin at high latitude, covered clothing, old age, malabsorption, liver or kidney disease, and enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants. Without calcitriol less calcium is absorbed, PTH rises (secondary hyperparathyroidism) and phosphate is lost, so newly laid bone matrix (osteoid) cannot mineralise. In children this is **rickets** (bowed legs, rachitic rosary at the costochondral junctions, craniotabes, widened wrists, delayed walking and teeth); in adults it is **osteomalacia** (diffuse bone pain, proximal weakness, waddling gait, Looser zones or pseudofractures). Alkaline phosphatase is high. Too much vitamin D (supplement excess) or calcitriol from granulomas (sarcoid, TB) and lymphomas causes hypercalcaemia with hypercalciuria.'],
table:{cols:['Rickets','Defect','Labs and clues'],rows:[
['Nutritional','too little vitamin D or calcium','low 25-OH D, high PTH, low or normal calcium, low phosphate, high ALP'],
['Vitamin D-dependent type 1','1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) mutation','normal 25-OH D, low calcitriol; responds to calcitriol'],
['Vitamin D-dependent type 2','vitamin D receptor resistance','calcitriol very high; often alopecia'],
['X-linked hypophosphataemic','PHEX mutation, so FGF23 is high','phosphate wasting, low phosphate, normal calcium and PTH, low or normal calcitriol; burosumab (anti-FGF23)']]},
s:[['path','Hypocalcaemia: perioral and fingertip tingling, cramps, tetany, Chvostek sign (facial twitch on tapping the facial nerve), Trousseau sign (carpal spasm when a cuff is inflated above systolic pressure), prolonged QT, laryngospasm and seizures. Check albumin: total calcium falls with low albumin while ionised calcium is normal. Alkalosis lowers ionised calcium.']]},
{h:'MEN syndromes',p:[
'The multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes are autosomal dominant, so one inherited hit in every cell lets tumours appear in several glands, often as hyperplasia first and at younger ages than sporadic tumours. **MEN1** is the loss of a tumour suppressor, **menin** (chromosome 11): a second hit in a gland is needed, as in Knudson\'s model, and parathyroid hyperplasia is usually the first sign. **MEN2A and 2B** are a gain of function in the **RET** proto-oncogene (chromosome 10), a receptor tyrosine kinase on neural crest cells: one mutant copy is enough, almost every carrier gets medullary thyroid cancer, and the thyroid is removed in childhood at an age set by the specific mutation.'],
table:{cols:['Syndrome','Gene','Tumours'],rows:[['MEN1','MEN1 (menin)','parathyroid hyperplasia or adenomas (about 90%), pituitary adenomas (prolactinoma commonest), pancreatic and duodenal endocrine tumours (gastrinoma, insulinoma); adrenal and carcinoid tumours less often'],['MEN2A','RET','medullary thyroid carcinoma, phaeochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia; sometimes cutaneous lichen amyloidosis and Hirschsprung disease'],['MEN2B','RET','medullary thyroid carcinoma (earliest and most aggressive), phaeochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas of lips and tongue, intestinal ganglioneuromatosis, marfanoid habitus; no parathyroid disease']]},
s:[['path','Screen relatives by gene test. In MEN2, always exclude a phaeochromocytoma before any operation, including the thyroidectomy, because anaesthesia can trigger a hypertensive crisis.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A 58-year-old woman has a kidney stone, constipation and low mood; calcium is high, phosphate low, PTH high and urinary calcium high. Diagnosis?',o:['Primary hyperparathyroidism','Malignancy (PTHrP)','Vitamin D toxicity','Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia'],a:0,d:'path',c:'calcium-pth',x:'An autonomous parathyroid adenoma keeps secreting PTH despite the high calcium: bone is resorbed, phosphate is lost in the urine, and the filtered calcium load overwhelms the tubule, so stones form. Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia also has high calcium with a non-suppressed PTH, but its urinary calcium is low; PTHrP from a cancer suppresses PTH.'},
{q:'A heavy smoker with a central cavitating lung mass has calcium 3.4 mmol/L and a suppressed PTH. Mediator?',o:['Calcitriol','PTHrP','Calcitonin','FGF23'],a:1,d:'path',c:'calcium-pth',x:'Squamous cell lung cancer (and renal, breast and bladder cancers) secretes PTH-related peptide, which acts on the PTH receptor to resorb bone and hold calcium in the kidney, while the real PTH is suppressed. Excess calcitriol comes from granulomas and lymphomas, not from squamous carcinoma.'},
{q:'Which vitamin D form should be measured to judge whether a housebound woman has enough stores?',o:['Cholecalciferol','1,25-(OH)₂ vitamin D','Ergocalciferol','25-OH vitamin D'],a:3,d:'bio',c:'vitamins',x:'25-Hydroxyvitamin D, made in the liver, circulates in large amounts with a half-life of weeks, so it reflects stores. Calcitriol is tightly controlled by PTH and can be normal or even high in deficiency, because the raised PTH drives 1α-hydroxylase.'},
{q:'A short, round-faced boy with short 4th and 5th metacarpals has low calcium, high phosphate and high PTH. Diagnosis?',o:['Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism','Hypoparathyroidism','Pseudohypoparathyroidism','Vitamin D deficiency'],a:2,d:'phys',c:'calcium-pth',x:'Pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1A is end-organ resistance to PTH from a maternally inherited GNAS (Gsα) mutation: PTH is high but kidney and bone ignore it, and the Albright body habitus appears. The paternally inherited form, pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism, gives the same body with normal calcium and PTH, because of imprinting.'},
{q:'A man has a gastrinoma, a prolactinoma and hypercalcaemia; his sister has the same. Mutated gene?',o:['MEN1','RET','VHL','SDHB'],a:0,d:'path',c:'men',x:'MEN1 is loss of the tumour suppressor menin (chromosome 11, autosomal dominant): parathyroid hyperplasia, usually the first sign, pituitary adenomas and pancreatic or duodenal endocrine tumours. Gain-of-function RET causes MEN2, with medullary thyroid cancer and phaeochromocytoma instead.'},
{q:'A confused, dehydrated woman with breast cancer has a calcium of 3.6 mmol/L. First treatment?',o:['Furosemide','Zoledronic acid','Calcitonin','IV 0.9% saline'],a:3,d:'pharm',c:'calcium-pth',x:'Hypercalcaemia causes a nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and vomiting, so patients are volume-depleted, and saline both corrects that and increases renal calcium excretion. Calcitonin acts within hours but briefly and zoledronic acid takes 2–4 days, so both follow; loop diuretics are added only once the volume is restored.'}
]};
